**Result:** the status is `ready`, so the profile can go ahead. The leading read is **Type 6** at medium confidence, with **Type 1** as the main rival. Types 4, 9 and 2 are still possible, and the record notes why each falls short. I flagged both the typing as contested and the sensitive allegations (the Guildhall racism, the death threats, the Forest School remarks). Those flags mean the draft will go to all six reviewers instead of the usual two. It passes the pipeline's own validator.

**What decided it:** his printed interviews alone fit a principled, self-critical One about as well as a Six. Two things tipped it:
- **His own explanation.** On Paloma Faith's podcast (March 2025) she asked whether he's "very preoccupied about being good and doing the right thing all the time." He said "Yeah, totally," and described people-pleasing as "trying to create safeness for yourself, safety for yourself."
- **The same doubt, every year from 2016 to 2026.** He keeps saying versions of "this is the one where people will find me out," "when are they going to realise that they really wanted the other guy?" and "if it all gets taken away, you don't feel that loss so keenly." He also leans on others to get through it, while being steady and moving toward real danger: the King Lear understudy night, Hamlet, the Snape threats.

**Evidence base:**
- **39 sources, all opened.** That's 23 print interviews from 2016 to 2026, testimony from Michaela Coel, Lucy Prebble, Jessie Buckley, Joe Barton, Lennie James and Saoirse Ronan, and a 2014 eyewitness review of the King Lear night.
- **About 3h50m of podcast and video audio**, which I transcribed on this machine.
